## Operation

The K4 test starts the name server, CAN server, terminal server, idle task, and a terminal command client.

The command client supports:

    tr <train> <speed>
    rv <train>
    sw <switch> <S|C>
    help
    q

For reliable lab testing, the K4 image also runs an automatic command test at boot. It covers:

    help
    unknown command
    invalid tr command
    tr 14 0
    tr 14 5
    tr 14 14
    tr 14 15
    tr 300 5
    invalid rv command
    rv 14
    rv 300
    invalid sw command
    sw 1 S
    sw 1 C
    sw 1 X
    sw 300 S

Valid commands should print `ok`. Invalid boundary/error cases should print either `failed`, `invalid ... command`, or `unknown command`.

Examples:

    tr 14 5
    rv 14
    sw 1 S
    sw 1 C
    q

## Kernel Structure

The kernel continues to use task descriptors, fixed-size stacks, priority-based ready queues, Send/Receive/Reply messaging, and AwaitEvent.

K4 adds two I/O server structures:

1. Terminal server:
   - provides Getc and Putc wrappers;
   - handles terminal output through Putc requests;
   - handles terminal input through a stable server-side Getc path;
   - keeps fixed-size circular buffers for RX/TX data;
   - blocks client Getc requests until input is available.

2. CAN server:
   - initializes SPI0 and the MCP2515 CAN controller;
   - provides CanSend and CanReceive wrappers;
   - keeps fixed-size queues for pending transmit frames, received frames, and blocked receive clients;
   - has RX/TX notifier tasks that use AwaitEvent(EVENT_TIMER) to avoid busy waiting while polling the MCP2515.

The train command client translates terminal commands into CS2 CAN frames.

## Protocol Notes

The shifted CS2 identifier-field values are:

    Lok Geschwindigkeit: CAN-ID value 0x08
    Lok Richtung:       CAN-ID value 0x0A
    Zubehör Schalten:   CAN-ID value 0x16

The code helper accepts the unshifted Märklin commands (`0x04`, `0x05`,
`0x0B`) and builds extended identifiers using:

    (command << 17) | 0x4711

The Track D locomotive controlled by this repository uses the same MM Loc-ID
for speed and direction:

    00 00 00 <train>

## Limitations

The final K4 implementation is structured as server-based I/O from the
user-task perspective: clients do not access devices directly and all
terminal/CAN operations go through I/O servers.  Terminal RX and TX use
notifier tasks plus bounded server queues, so an idle `Getc` or full output
queue does not stall unrelated clients.  The MCP2515 CAN path uses periodic
`AwaitEvent(EVENT_TIMER)` notifier polling rather than a direct MCP2515
hardware interrupt line, because the stable kernel exception path does not
yet include a reliable external interrupt-controller path.

The implementation uses fixed-size queues. If a queue is full, the relevant server call returns an error.

